---
import Docs from '../../../layouts/Docs.astro'
import DocTable from '../../../components/DocTable.astro'
import RecipeCard from '../../../components/RecipeCard.astro'

const macro = `defaults:
  who: sam@example.com
steps:
  - fill: { label: Email }
    value: $who
  - click: { role: button, name: Sign in }`

const use = `setup:
  - use: sign-in
    with: { who: admin@example.com }`

const rawData = `open:
  - name: Acme Corp
    reference: INV-1042
shipped:
  - name: Globex
    reference: INV-1043`

const each = `setup:
  - each: $orders.open
    as: order
    steps:
      - fill: { label: Search }
        value: \${order.name}`
---

<Docs
  title="Macros and data files reference"
  description="The macro file format, how data files enter scope, and the rules for variable references."
>
  <h1 class="text-4xl font-extrabold">Macros and data files</h1>

  <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">Macro files</h2>

  <p>
    One file per macro in <code>paths.macros</code>. The filename is the macro's name. A macro
    cannot contain <code>clip</code>, <code>marks</code> or <code>callouts</code>.
  </p>

  <DocTable
    head={['Key', 'Default', 'Effect']}
    rows={[
      ['<code>steps</code>', 'required', 'The steps to run'],
      [
        '<code>defaults</code>',
        '<code>{}</code>',
        'Values the macro reads, overridable by the caller',
      ],
    ]}
  />

  <div class="mt-6">
    <RecipeCard code={macro} file="screenshots/macros/sign-in.yaml" />
  </div>

  <p>Call it from a recipe or another macro with the <code>use</code> step:</p>

  <div class="mt-6">
    <RecipeCard code={use} file="screenshots/recipes/dashboard.yaml" />
  </div>

  <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">Data files</h2>

  <p>
    Every file in <code>paths.data</code> is in scope, named after its filename:
    <code>orders.yaml</code> is <code>$orders</code>. A filename must contain only letters, digits
    and underscores, because a reference stops at a hyphen.
  </p>

  <div class="mt-6">
    <RecipeCard code={rawData} file="screenshots/data/orders.yaml" />
  </div>

  <div class="mt-6">
    <RecipeCard code={each} file="screenshots/recipes/orders.yaml" />
  </div>

  <h2 id="references" class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">References</h2>

  <DocTable
    head={['Form', 'Meaning']}
    rows={[
      ['<code>$name</code>', 'The value bound to <code>name</code>'],
      ['<code>{"${name}"}</code>', 'The same, delimited'],
      ['<code>$a.b</code>', 'A path into the value bound to <code>a</code>'],
      ['<code>{"${env.NAME}"}</code>', 'An environment variable the run was allowed to read'],
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    A name continues through letters, digits, underscores and dots, so braces are required where the
    reference runs into text that could belong to it: <code>{'Order ${order.name}.'}</code> ends the reference
    before the full stop, while <code>Order $order.name.</code> looks for a path ending in a dot.
  </p>

  <h3>Where values come from</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>data files, by filename</li>
    <li><code>with</code> on a <code>use</code> step, for the macro it calls</li>
    <li><code>readValue: … as: name</code>, for the steps that follow it</li>
    <li><code>each: … as: name</code>, for its own steps only</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Rules</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      A reference that is the whole value keeps that value's type. A reference inside a longer
      string becomes text.
    </li>
    <li>
      References are substituted in steps only, never in <code>clip</code>, <code>marks</code> or <code
        >callouts</code
      >.
    </li>
    <li>A reference that matches nothing is an error, not an empty string.</li>
    <li>A literal <code>$</code> inside a longer string is left alone.</li>
    <li>
      <code>__proto__</code>, <code>constructor</code> and <code>prototype</code> resolve to nothing.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>{'${env.NAME}'}</code> resolves only for names given to <code>--allow-env</code> or listed
      in <code>SHOTLIST_ENV</code>; an <code>--untrusted</code> run resolves none.
    </li>
  </ul>
</Docs>
